  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 scholarly book is already showing early uptake: OpenAlex records 1 citation, and the work has accumulated 448 downloads through OAPEN, suggesting active reading beyond initial publication. The citation appears in a 2024 book chapter, indicating that the book has begun to enter specialist discussion in Renaissance and historical philosophy of science.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Pietro Pomponazzi and the Renaissance Theory of the Elements presents an early but credible impact profile for a specialist humanities monograph. The strongest evidence is usage: 448 OAPEN downloads point to sustained interest in the book since publication, while the single OpenAlex citation shows that the work is beginning to be taken up in adjacent scholarship. Because the citation base is still very small, the record should be read as the start of scholarly recognition rather than as a mature citation pattern.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is narrow but informative. The one citing work is a 2024 book chapter in a venue focused on medieval and early modern medicine, which fits the book\u2019s subject matter and suggests relevance to historians of philosophy, science, and the Renaissance. The citing work\u2019s classification in history-linked concepts reinforces that the book is being used as a source within closely related humanities research rather than in a diffuse, general audience context.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The publication also has signs of formal dissemination and discoverability. It is listed as open access in a hybrid model, appears in OpenAIRE with multiple repository instances, and is associated with verified funding from KU Leuven and the University of Siegen. Those features matter for reach because they make the book easier to find, access, and reuse in teaching and research settings.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Evidence for wider public conversation is currently thin: the record does not show event coverage or social media discussion in the data provided. Teaching adoption is not directly evidenced here beyond library holdings, so the safest interpretation is that the book has achieved early scholarly circulation, with usage stronger than citation count would yet suggest. Overall, the record supports a narrative of a newly published specialist book that is already being read and cited within its field, with additional impact likely still emerging.</p>",
